tiply into one listing's takings. For the average listing, a 10% higher rate on the nights it already books adds about $2,250 a year. Open for the busy months. In a typical year, July brings the average listing about $6,800, against $1,340 in January, so a calendar that is open and full in the busy months carries the year. Reviews that stand out. Myrtle Beach listings average 4.66 stars, and 23% carry Airbnb's Guest Favorite badge, which guests can filter for. What a listing keeps comes down to the figures this page measures: nightly rate, nights booked and the months it's open. A listing at the top-quarter mark takes in about $3,690 a month, against $2,150 for the average one. Occupancy What Is the Airbnb Occupancy Rate in Myrtle Beach? Myrtle Beach listings were booked on 36% of the nights they were open over the past year, 3 percentage points below the South Carolina average (39%) and 5 points below the U.S. average (41%). In a typical year, occupancy swings from 26% in January to 56% in July. The busiest tenth of listings are booked about 4.2 times as often as the bottom quarter. Best-in-class properties (top 10%) are booked 74%+ of open nights, 40 points above the median. Strong performers (top 25%) fill 56% or more, roughly four nights a week. Typical properties (median) fill about 34% of open nights. Entry-level properties (bottom 25%) fill 18% or less, under two nights a week. July is usually the busiest month in Myrtle Beach, January the quietest Share of open nights booked, by month, September 2024 to August 2026 0% 20% 40% 60% Monthly average 40% 58% 24% Nov 24 Feb 25 May 25 Aug 25 Nov 25 Feb 26 May 26 Aug 26 The 12-month rate of 36% averages each listing's whole year, slow months included; each month here counts only the listings booked in it. Nightly rates How Much Does an Airbnb Cost per Night in Myrtle Beach? The average nightly rate in Myrtle Beach, known as ADR (average daily rate), was $257 over the past year, about 28% below the South Carolina ave